Author **Ambient Snowfall**, a 128x128 screen-space particle effect, using only
the particle tool.

It is a gentle, looping **weather layer**: many soft white snowflakes of varied
sizes drifting slowly downward and swaying side to side on a light breeze, with
smaller flakes falling slower for a sense of depth. Calm and wintry, it is meant to
sit continuously behind or in front of a scene. You are authoring a **planar,
screen-space** effect (width and height only, no depth).

## How the tool works

You do **not** place individual particles. You **author a system** — emitters,
forces, and per-particle curves — that the review UI and a game **simulate live**,
the way a real particle editor (Niagara, VFX Graph) plays a system. The only way to
shape the effect is the `particle-2d` binary already on your `PATH`. It is the sole
channel: you build the system by calling it **one operation at a time**, and the
ordered list of operations you issue — recorded to `actions.json` — is the
**authoritative output that is scored**, together with the system it emits. Anything
produced any other way is discarded.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags. Run `particle-2d --help` to
list every operation and `particle-2d <operation> --help` for one operation's exact
flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. The field dimensions, the
duration, and the fps are already seeded alongside the workspace at `{{workspace}}`,
so no operation needs those flags.

Rendering is a separate, on-request step: run **`particle-2d render`** to simulate
the system over its duration, write the preview GIF, and **emit the `system.json`**
the result is built from. Render to check your progress — and, crucially, **before
you finish**, so the system is not left empty. Because the effect is simulated live
and loops, it varies slightly from play to play; author it to read the same across
replays and to loop seamlessly.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Author the emitter system operation by operation until `particle-2d render` plays
the effect the brief describes — a calm, continuous field of soft white flakes of
varied sizes drifting down and swaying on a light breeze, smaller flakes slower for
parallax, looping seamlessly over the window. Run `render` to emit the system, then
stop: the recorded `actions.json` and the emitted `system.json` are your finished
submission.
